Print the startup stage, not the address

Three rounds went into the address: it resolved to a static function with no
symbol, and the seven digit argument could not be read off the panel reliably
(glare, and two log sources drawing over each other).  What is actually needed
is the stage, so print subsystem and rank instead -- both are small numbers that
survive a poor photo.

  [#836:9.1]   entry 836, subsystem 9 (PERCPU), rank 1

Ranks of UINT32_MAX (STARTUP_RANK_LAST) collapse to 99 to keep the line short.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -407,6 +407,20 @@ kernel_startup_initialize_upto(startup_subsystem_id_t upto)
 		}
 		startup_phase = cur->subsystem - 1;
 		kernel_startup_log(cur->subsystem);
+		/*
+		 * nio: print the stage rather than the function address.  The
+		 * address resolved to an unnamed static function, and the argument
+		 * could not be read off the panel reliably; subsystem and rank are
+		 * small numbers that survive a poor photo.
+		 */
+		if (cur->subsystem >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
+			static uint32_t nio_entry_no;
+			uint32_t nio_this = ++nio_entry_no;
+			if ((nio_this & 7u) == 0u) {
+				printf("\n[#%u:%u.%u]", nio_this, cur->subsystem,
+				    (unsigned)(cur->rank % 100u));
+			}
+		}
 		cur->func(cur->arg);
 		startup_entry_cur = ++cur;
 	}
